     Essentially, the problem with wands of polymorph is synergism, right?
You polymorph rocks into dilithium crystals, wands of opening into wands of
wishing.
     I think having a % destruction chance isn't the best way to go; instead
of eliminating the problem, one is trying to balance it out, and can end
up under- or overly succeeding there.
     I think what should be done is simply to assign each object type (for
instance, a wand of light) a "usefulness" value.  Thus, for wands, an
incomplete list would be something like:
opening 1
light 2
wishing 10
death 10
fire 7
cold 6
magic missle 4
(etc.)
     A blessed item is +1 on the chart, a cursed item - (I may be unclear
on the polymorph bit, but don't items randomly lose and gain cursed
status during them?  Blessings too?  If they retain status, forget that
bit).  Thus a blessed class 9 wand (perhaps a blessed wand of polymorph)
could be polymorphed into a wand of wishing; an uncursed wand of polymorph
could be polymorphed into a cursed wand of wishing.  (If one was lucky,
of course.)
     One could add a randomness factor with a distribution of something
on the order of:
10% +2
20% +1
40% 0
20% -1
10% -2
     That way synergistic effects would be reduced greatly.  You'd
have no chance of getting a wand of wishing from a wand of digging
(which might be class 6 or so; items with less types could be on a
scale of 1-5 rather than 1-10, or something else entirely.)-- in
two polymorphs, roughly 1 in 100 wands of digging would even make it
to class 10, and then there are still wands of death and such to
compete with them.
     More on cursing/blessing:
Basically, the process would be something like:
1) Determine value of object (wand of polymorph = 9)
2) Degrade/upgrade by cursing (it's cursed; class 8 now)
3) Include random factor (bad luck; now it's class 6)
4) Determine whether new item will be blessed or cursed-- now we have
   to reverse the effect (produce a cursed class 7 item or a blessed
   class 5).  (Blessed; we have a class 5 item now)
5) Determine object by random sampling of class 5 items (congratulations;
   you now have a blessed wand of make invisible, perhaps).  Before this
   step, we'd have to min(x,10) and max(x,1) to make sure it's on the
   table.  (Alternatively, if one ended up with a 0 or lower, the item
   would be gone, and if one ended up with above a 10, one would get
   an either free or restricted [to level of item] wish as to which type.)

     The only problem I can think of is that that random +- factor might
be too large for the scales that some items require (e.g. shields, gauntlets).
(Well, bad examples, since that's all grouped up in "armor."  But gems, for
instance [still a bad example].)  Well, hell, then, THERE ARE NO PROBLEMS!
(Just kidding.)

     This may seem complicated, but really, all it requires is a new piece
of data on every object-type's definition (possibly replacing another
already existing piece, since wands of wishing seem to be quite scarce
on polymorph piles to start with, which is probaly due to some distribution
info in its declaration) and a not-too-tough piece of code in the polymorph
section (finding class <x> items is probably the toughest part, and if one
can already find an object by typing in its name in several forms, it
can't be too tough to do that...).  No heroic feats like the "time-travel"
trap that's recently been proposed.

     So, this would completely eliminate turning rocks into dilithium
crystals and such (if we call rocks class 1 and crystals class 10, it would
take some 4 +2s and a +1 to push them up that far; a 1 in 50,000 chance
for that to happen initially, and at least five polymorphs as well) while
not completely destroying piles of objects (like that 1/1-n thing-- after
two polymorphs of a stack, the stack is half gone!  In fact, after n
polymorphs, you've got only Q[n] = 1/n(Q[0]) items left!  The purpose of
polymorphing is completely defeated.)
     Which brings us back to the question of what *is* the purpose of
polymorphing items?  I would say that it's to take items which your class
and circumstance has little use for and changing it into comparable items
which might be slightly more useful (for instance, one might have two pairs
of gauntlets of power, in which case by this system one could polymorph it
into a comparably powerful item such as as helm of brilliance or boots of
speed).  Thus a great find, assuming one has a wand of polymorph, is still
a great find even if you've already found it.

     Well, hope this helps...

